WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.082 --> 00:00:06.290
PETER DYKSTRA: Hey, everyone.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:09.160
My name is Peter, and I'm part
of the AppSheet product team

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.200
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.870
Thanks for joining this
session about how to build AI

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.510
powered no-code
apps with AppSheet.

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:20.450
I personally work on making
AppSheet as accessible

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:22.520
as possible for
business users that

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:26.310
don't have coding experience
to build apps with AppSheet.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.360
And I'm joined by
my teammate, Tony,

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.010
who is a senior engineer
focused on building AI

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.860
into the platform to
make it easier to use,

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.560
as well as into the end
applications that people

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.920
can create to make those apps
themselves really powerful.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.417
Thanks for joining, Tony.

00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:42.760
TONY FADER: Hey, Peter.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:43.600
Thanks.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.880
So today we're going to walk
through the basics of how

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.820
to build an application
without writing code in order

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.465
to digitize a common
business process.

00:00:51.465 --> 00:00:53.590
After we do that, we're
going to look at a few ways

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.500
to make the app intelligent and
even more efficient by adding

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.458
machine learning to it.

00:00:57.458 --> 00:00:58.333
PETER DYKSTRA: Great.

00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:00.910
So for a little background,
a real serious challenge

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.820
that many businesses face
is this growing demand

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.250
for improving their
internal technology,

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.860
but they frequently lack
the skills and resources

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.090
to update and build those
solutions internally.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:14.980
Software that comes
out of the box

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.620
rarely meets all the
unique requirements

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.350
that a business has, as
developing custom solutions

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.130
becomes necessary real quickly.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.670
But it's expensive,
it's time-consuming,

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.022
and frequently just
doesn't get done.

00:01:27.022 --> 00:01:28.480
There just aren't
enough developers

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.450
to help tackle all the
opportunities for updating

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.892
and modernizing the process.

00:01:34.892 --> 00:01:35.600
TONY FADER: Yeah.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.810
And it's even harder to use AI
and machine learning to make

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.570
your business workflow smarter.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.060
The skill sets for
these are rare.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.220
It involves unique
infrastructure

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.310
for building your applications,
and configuring these things

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:50.820
just takes tons of time.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.210
And so this is just overhead
that most organizations

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.990
don't have.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:00.340
So intelligent no-code
platforms help solve this

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.830
by enabling anyone to
be that app developer AI

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:06.730
expert, no matter what
their background is.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:10.090
So machine learning baked
into the no-code platform

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.620
helps in three ways.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.360
First, it makes
app creation easier

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.990
for the user by guiding them
along the common pathways.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:19.330
Second, it makes the apps
themselves more powerful

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.000
and efficient by, say,
automatically doing

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.590
data entry for you.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:27.790
And third, it makes governance
even easier and more reliable

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:30.730
in the long term.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:32.550
So no-code apps can
include all sorts

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.620
of powerful
functionality, including

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.110
collecting or managing
data more efficiently,

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.590
automating business workflows,
and sharing insights

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.780
with teammates, taking
advantage of things

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.970
like business intelligence
with dashboards, charts,

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:46.470
and reports.

00:02:49.937 --> 00:02:50.770
PETER DYKSTRA: Yeah.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.370
There's opportunity
for creating apps

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.410
that use features
like those really all

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.170
throughout an organization.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.080
It could be out in the
field with customers.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.780
It could be managing projects.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.590
It could be like manufacturing
or distribution facilities,

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:06.530
or in the office in a
more corporate setting.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:08.930
But some of the most
valuable opportunities

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:10.910
are with desk-less workers.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:12.410
So these teammates
don't necessarily

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.810
sit behind a computer
during the day.

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.300
They're more likely to
experience friction trying

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.610
to collaborate with
other teammates

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.250
or access important
information just in order

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.968
to do their job effectively.

00:03:24.968 --> 00:03:26.760
So let's look at this
example of how to get

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.560
started building a
no-code application that

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.160
helps a manufacturing team
perform quality control

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.320
inspections more efficiently
from their mobile devices.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.140
Specifically, let's
digitize their inspections,

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:44.510
taking it from a pen and
paper process to a mobile app.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.730
And then Tony can
help take that app

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.710
and make it even more
efficient by building

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:53.430
a machine learning model into
it with just a few steps.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.530
So how do you get started?

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.670
To start the process, you're
connecting a data source

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:03.100
to AppSheet to serve as the
back end of that application.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.350
Then we'll define an
interface and behavior

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:08.800
of that app in AppSheet before
we share it with the team.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.590
Once it's in the
hands of end users,

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.780
those updates that they're
making in the application

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:17.519
get synced right back to
that original data source.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.850
So let's open up
AppSheet and get started

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:21.595
building this particular app.

00:04:21.595 --> 00:04:22.970
In order to get
this app started,

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:23.840
we want to make
sure that we have

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.370
the data source ready to go.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:28.430
In this case, we're going
to use a Google Sheet here,

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.290
and this Google Sheet
has three tables.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:33.080
We have some recent
inspection data,

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.252
we have the parts that
are being inspected,

00:04:35.252 --> 00:04:36.710
and we also have
the employees that

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.910
are performing the inspections.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:40.580
So let's connect
this to AppSheet

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.915
to get this app started.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:52.392
We're going to
connect to Google,

00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:54.600
and we're going to find this
particular Google Sheet.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.420
And AppSheet's going to connect
to that initial inspections

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:00.920
table.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.125
Then we'll want to connect
to the other two as well.

00:05:03.125 --> 00:05:05.000
While it's connecting
to those tables, what's

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.190
happening behind the
scenes is that it's

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.770
looking at the
content of that data

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:13.910
and then building a relevant
prototype automatically

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.640
based on what it can find.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:19.820
And so you can see we already
have an application here

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:24.500
that allows for creating
new inspections for viewing

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:27.910
those existing
ones, and then also

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:30.850
viewing the inspections
on a calendar based

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:34.710
on the dates in that data.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:37.520
Let's connect the other
two tables, and then

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.270
let's customize this interface.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:54.010
With these three
tables connected now,

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:56.860
one thing that AppSheet was
doing also behind the scenes

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.320
was intelligently
building relationships

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.720
between the tables based
on what it could find,

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:05.170
based on the fields that it's
looking at in these tables.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:08.160
And so because we
have a relationship--

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.920
there are employees
performing the inspections,

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.745
and the inspections are being
performed on different parts--

00:06:14.745 --> 00:06:16.120
there's going to
be relationships

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:18.430
between this data,
and that's already

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:21.290
represented in this
prototype of the application.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:23.800
If we look at one of
these inspections,

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:28.630
you can see that the part
itself has been connected

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:31.480
and I can navigate
to the part details.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:33.040
When I'm looking
at this part, it

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:37.300
has related inspections that
have been performed on it.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.070
I can also see the
employee who performed

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:40.210
this particular inspection.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.920
I can go to that
employee's details.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:47.960
So with these
relationships established,

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:49.910
that just gives me
a huge head start

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:53.780
on what type of functionality I
can expose in the application,

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:57.590
and it makes it a lot
easier then to now customize

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.200
the app to be exactly what
I want for my end users.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.240
If I move into the
UX section, you

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.490
can see that there were a
few different views created,

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:11.210
but I want this
application to be fairly

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:13.110
straightforward and simple.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:16.280
So I'm going to go ahead and
remove a couple of these views,

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:24.010
and I'm going to customize
the inspections view

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:29.640
and make sure that there is a
dedicated form view to create

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:31.395
new inspection entries.

00:07:35.785 --> 00:07:37.160
You can see
throughout the editor

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:40.810
there are going to be
suggestions for what features

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:46.750
and what elements might be
helpful in your application.

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:50.120
In this case, when I'm
looking at the view types,

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:53.110
I have an automatic view
type that has been selected.

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:55.110
I'd like to take advantage
of these images that

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:58.107
are being collected from
these inspection cards.

00:07:58.107 --> 00:07:59.940
So I'm going to use the
card view, actually,

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.070
to better highlight
those images.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:08.808
What's advantageous
about this card view

00:08:08.808 --> 00:08:10.350
is that it provides
a really easy way

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:15.720
of defining which fields get
exposed in the app interface.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.970
And so here, I'm just
going to go through some

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:19.620
of these different fields.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.970
Maybe I want to show the
employee and the date

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:23.310
of their inspection up top.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.540
And then the main image
is already selected.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:33.330
And then I really want to
highlight whether or not

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:37.900
this inspection was
accepted or rejected,

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:42.130
and so I'll make that
the primary header.

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:44.740
And then I want to make sure
that, if it is rejected,

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:51.430
the description of that
inspection gets highlighted.

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:54.310
Now, I can include
actions in this view

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:58.628
to help navigate or
initiate workflows.

00:08:58.628 --> 00:09:00.670
I'm going to remove those
so it's nice and clean.

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:02.830
And so now I have a
view here on the right

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:05.530
showing those existing
inspections that

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:10.070
have been performed that I'm
starting this application with.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:14.520
Last step here is, because
these are inspection cards,

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:18.520
I'll put a relevant
view icon of a card.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:22.970
And I'd like, alongside this,
a quick way for my application

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:26.060
users to perform
new inspections.

00:09:29.213 --> 00:09:30.630
When creating this
view, I'm going

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:33.210
to make sure that it is
adding new inspections

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.320
to the inspections table.

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:36.640
I'm going to make this a form.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:44.500
And then I'll make this form
icon something relevant,

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:48.630
like a magnifying
glass and a plus sign.

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:49.130
Great.

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:51.900
So now this application
consists of two primary views--

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:55.620
a way of adding new inspections,
and a way of viewing existing

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:56.970
inspections.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:58.670
I'm going to go ahead
and save this app.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:06.750
I want to make sure the app
logo is very relevant so my app

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:08.670
users recognize the application.

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.880
And then, because an important
part of this application

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:26.410
is really quickly
identifying inspections

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:28.150
that have been
rejected, I'm going

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:30.220
to add a format rule
that just calls attention

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:31.930
to rejected inspections.

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:38.720
So in this case, I'm going
to say, for every inspection

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:47.480
where the condition
equals rejected,

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.990
I'm going to apply a format
to that particular condition

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:51.620
field.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:55.490
And in this case, I want a--

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:59.852
let's see-- little alert, and
I want the text to be red.

00:10:59.852 --> 00:11:01.560
And you can already
see here on the right

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.990
that we're previewing
that effect.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:05.880
So I'll go ahead and save this.

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:07.230
Great.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:10.440
So now I'll open this
application up and preview it

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:11.460
in tablet form.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:15.090
And you can get a better sense
for how those inspections that

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:19.400
have been logged
by my employees are

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:21.870
visible and readily accessible.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:24.740
And I can also very
quickly get to the details

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:27.110
of the different employees
and see inspections

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:31.710
that they have performed,
or, for example,

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:34.530
look at different parts
that are being manufactured

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:36.150
and see related inspections.

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:42.650
So one reason why this
is valuable to employees

00:11:42.650 --> 00:11:44.270
is that it's
digitizing the process,

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:47.360
and it's making this
information more

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:49.860
reliably accessible long term.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:52.370
Another really important
part of this application

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:56.690
is getting the information into
the hands of the right people

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:59.270
more quickly than
it previously did.

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:01.010
So one way that we
can support that is

00:12:01.010 --> 00:12:04.270
by building a workflow
in order to notify

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:06.250
the right person
or the right team

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:09.757
as soon as an inspection
has been logged as rejected.

00:12:09.757 --> 00:12:11.590
So in this case, let's
build a workflow that

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:16.030
sends a notification email
to the right contact whenever

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:21.418
a new inspection has been logged
that meets this condition.

00:12:21.418 --> 00:12:22.960
In this case, we're
going to continue

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:24.850
looking at that
inspections table,

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:28.540
and we're going to say that
every time a new row has been

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:39.070
added and the condition
equals rejected,

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:42.810
we're going to send an email,
and we'll send this email

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:44.970
to the operations team.

00:12:48.673 --> 00:12:50.340
And we'll want a
relevant email subject.

00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:59.020
And then we can actually
use a Google Doc

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:02.690
as the template for this email.

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:05.680
So on the side here,
I've created a Google Doc

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:08.680
that is just a simple
layout that includes some

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:12.880
of the fields from my app data.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:17.690
I can use this as a template
for this particular email.

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:21.540
And so if I include a link
to that template, every time

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:24.870
this workflow will run, it
will draw from that template

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:27.960
and send an email formatted
based on that Google Doc

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:28.990
layout.

00:13:28.990 --> 00:13:29.490
Great.

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:33.120
Now let's test what happens
when we add a new inspection

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:34.830
to this application.

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:37.260
We can use a photo of a
recently inspected tag that's

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:40.530
come off one of the parts.

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:42.470
This part has been
marked as rejected,

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:44.220
and I'll fill in some
of the details here.

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.680
Submitting a form
in an AppSheet app

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.360
passes that data right through
to the connected data source.

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:14.760
So in this case, it's going to
add a row to the Google Sheet,

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:17.190
and that added row
triggers the workflow

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:20.260
that will send the email
notification to the team.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:26.180
So at this point, the app is
ready to start being used,

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:27.950
and I can get it to
the team by sending

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:30.080
individual email invites.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:34.400
I can invite just a group
from my organization,

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:37.500
or I can make it accessible
to the entire domain,

